<java version="1.8.0_492-internal" class="java.beans.XMLDecoder">
 <int>3</int>
 <string>&lt;html&gt;
  &lt;head&gt;
    &lt;style type=&quot;text/css&quot;&gt;
      &lt;!--
        body { font-size: 16pt }
      --&gt;
    &lt;/style&gt;
    
  &lt;/head&gt;
  &lt;body&gt;
    &lt;h2&gt;
      Exercises to accompany Tutorial 6
    &lt;/h2&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      5/5/15
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;h3&gt;
      Exercise 1 (of 3)
    &lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      Each of the derivations in the following Exercise can be done using the 
      Rule of Elimination of the Conditional &amp;#8835;E, and the rules learned 
      previously.
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      If a formula in a line of a derivation has the form
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p style=&quot;margin-left: 40px&quot;&gt;
      &amp;lt;formula1&amp;gt;
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      and a formula in a line of a derivation has the form
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p style=&quot;margin-left: 40px&quot;&gt;
      &amp;lt;formula1&amp;gt; &amp;#8835; &amp;lt;formula2&amp;gt;
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      the Rule of Elimination of the Conditional &amp;#8835;E allows you to add
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p style=&quot;margin-left: 40px&quot;&gt;
      &amp;lt;formula2&amp;gt;
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      The elimination of &amp;#8835; {&amp;#8835;E} is the classical inference rule known as Modus 
      Ponens. In English, if you have A and If A then B you are entitled to B.
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      Show each of the following arguments to be valid.
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;blockquote&gt;
      (a) F, F&amp;#8835;G &amp;#8756; G
    &lt;/blockquote&gt;
    &lt;blockquote&gt;
      (b) F&amp;#8835;G, F &amp;#8756; G&amp;amp;F
    &lt;/blockquote&gt;
    &lt;blockquote&gt;
      (c) F&amp;#8835;G, G&amp;#8835;H,F &amp;#8756;H
    &lt;/blockquote&gt;
    &lt;blockquote&gt;
      (d) F&amp;#8835;(G&amp;#8835;H), F&amp;#8835;G,F &amp;#8756;H&amp;amp;F
    &lt;/blockquote&gt;
    &lt;blockquote&gt;
      (e) F&amp;#8835;((~G)&amp;#8835;H), F&amp;#8835;~G,F &amp;#8756;H&amp;amp;F
    &lt;/blockquote&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;h3&gt;
      Exercise 2 (of 3)
    &lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      Each of the derivations in the following Exercise can be done using only 
      the rule the Rule of Elimination of the Biconditional &amp;#8801;E, and the rules 
      learned previously.
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      If the formula in a line in a derivation has the form
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p style=&quot;margin-left: 40px&quot;&gt;
      &amp;lt;formula1&amp;gt;&amp;#8801;&amp;lt;formula2&amp;gt;
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      the Rule of Elimination of the Biconditional &amp;#8801;E allows you to remove the 
      biconditional and add either
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p style=&quot;margin-left: 40px&quot;&gt;
      &amp;lt;formula1&amp;gt;&amp;#8835;&amp;lt;formula2&amp;gt;
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      or
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p style=&quot;margin-left: 40px&quot;&gt;
      &amp;lt;formula2&amp;gt;&amp;#8835;&amp;lt;formula1&amp;gt;
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      as an extra line. You have a choice here (on the direction of the 
      conditional).
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      Show each of the following arguments to be valid.
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;blockquote&gt;
      (a) F&amp;#8801;G &amp;#8756; G&amp;#8835;F&lt;br&gt;(b) F&amp;#8801;G &amp;#8756; F&amp;#8835;G&lt;br&gt;(c) 
      F&amp;#8801;G, G &amp;#8756; H&amp;#8744;F&lt;br&gt;(d) F&amp;#8801;G, G&amp;#8801;H, F &amp;#8756; H
    &lt;/blockquote&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;h3&gt;
      Exercise 3 (of 3).
    &lt;/h3&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      After you have started the proof switch on &apos;Tactics&apos; under the Proof &lt;em&gt;Wizard&lt;/em&gt; 
      menu, and do the derivations in the following way. If any of the lines &lt;i&gt;you 
      wish to obtain&lt;/i&gt; has a connective in it, click the appropriate 
      Introduction rule (in these examples this will split the problem in 
      two). If any of the lines &lt;i&gt;you have obtained&lt;/i&gt; has a connective in 
      it, use an Elimination rule to take it out.
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;
      
    &lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;blockquote&gt;
      (a) F&amp;#8801;G, H&amp;amp;G &amp;#8756;G&amp;amp;F
    &lt;/blockquote&gt;
    &lt;blockquote&gt;
      (b) (F&amp;amp;H)&amp;amp;(R&amp;amp;G) &amp;#8756;F&amp;amp;G
    &lt;/blockquote&gt;
    &lt;hr&gt;
  &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</string>
